<h2>Opting Out of AI Training in One robots.txt Line — The Shared Token Four Companies Back, and This Wiki's Choice</h2>

<h3>Three-line summary</h3>
<ul>
    <li>On October 30 an Anthropic engineer submitted an individual draft to the IETF. It proposes a single token, <code>__usage_ai-training</code>, for stating whether a site's content may be used to train AI. The same day Google, Microsoft and OpenAI said they would honor the signal.</li>
    <li>Until now a site had to block each company's own name (GPTBot, Google-Extended, ClaudeBot and so on). The new token names a <strong>use</strong>, not a crawler. But <code>User-agent: *</code> does not match it, and a file without a token group is read as &ldquo;no preference stated&rdquo;.</li>
    <li>This wiki made the opposite choice. On November 2 it added <code>Allow: /</code> under the token in its <code>robots.txt</code>, so it now says separately that <strong>AI training is welcome</strong>, not just crawling.</li>
</ul>

<p><code>robots.txt</code> is a small text file at the top path of a website. When an automated collector (a crawler) from a search engine or an AI company arrives, it reads this file first to see which paths it may fetch. The convention dates from 1994, and in 2022 the IETF (the international body that writes internet technical standards) wrote its syntax down as RFC 9309. The trouble is that the file was only ever meant to say &ldquo;may you come in.&rdquo; &ldquo;May you train AI on what you take&rdquo; is a different question, and so far sites have answered it indirectly by blocking crawler names each company chose for itself. The new draft gives that second question a shared place to live.</p>

<h3>One use instead of many name tags</h3>
<p>A site that wanted to keep its content out of AI training had to know each company's name. OpenAI uses GPTBot; Google uses Google-Extended, a token kept separate from its search crawler; Anthropic uses ClaudeBot; Meta uses meta-externalagent. Microsoft has treated the NoArchive and NoCache page tags as its signal for limiting AI use. Every new company meant another line, and a crawler you had never heard of could not be blocked.</p>
<p>The new token is named after a <strong>purpose</strong>, AI training, rather than a particular crawler. The syntax is an ordinary robots.txt group with no new directive, so existing parsers read it as is. To keep the whole site out of training but leave a press folder open, a site writes:</p>
<pre><code>User-agent: __usage_ai-training
Disallow: /
Allow: /press-kit/</code></pre>
<p>To say the opposite, that everything may be used for training, one <code>Allow: /</code> line is enough. That is the form this wiki added.</p>
<pre><code>User-agent: __usage_ai-training
Allow: /</code></pre>

<h3>How a crawler reads the token</h3>
<p>The draft's rules are short, but two of them cut against old robots.txt habits. First, the <code>User-agent: *</code> group, which addresses every crawler, does not apply to this token. Second, if the token group is missing, the file is treated as neither allowing nor refusing but as <strong>&ldquo;no preference stated&rdquo;</strong>. On the day the draft appeared, that described every robots.txt in the world.</p>
