<p>The signal is a <strong>preference</strong>. It is not an order that stops a crawler from fetching pages, so crawling for search indexes can continue. Nor is it retroactive: it covers content fetched while the file is in place and does not pull anything out of training sets or models that already exist. Several reports also note that a private agreement can take precedence over the signal.</p>
<p>The draft leaves the order between the shared token and company-specific rules to each company. Google says that if a site refuses training with the shared token and does not name Google-Extended, it will treat that as a Google-Extended disallow. If a separate Google-Extended rule exists, that rule wins. So a combination such as &ldquo;refuse in general, but allow Google&rdquo; is possible.</p>

