<h3>Microsoft splits one step further: training and grounding</h3>
<p>Microsoft's announcement has a setting the other three lack. <strong>Grounding (AI Grounding)</strong> means an AI fetching a web page on the spot and using it as a source while composing an answer. That is a different use from training a model in advance. Microsoft takes the training preference through the shared token and the grounding preference through a separate page-level setting. A site can then let Copilot or Bing's AI answers cite it while still refusing training.</p>
<p>The two settings replace the NoArchive and NoCache tags. Microsoft will honor both while crawling from February 1, 2027. A site that has used the old tags to block grounding must publish the new setting on its pages and record a temporary preference in Bing Webmaster Tools by March 1; otherwise grounding defaults to allowed until the page is recrawled. Meta tags and HTTP headers that override the robots.txt setting for a single page are also provided.</p>
<p>Google has not said whether AI Overviews or AI Mode are affected by the signal. Keeping search visibility and training apart matters most to sites such as news publishers that want to appear in search but not to be used for training.</p>

<h3>The distance left to a standard</h3>
<p>The document is an IETF <strong>individual draft</strong>. Anyone can submit one, and it has no standing as a standard until a working group adopts it and reaches consensus. The IETF's document tracker marks it as not endorsed by the IETF, and it expires on May 3, 2027. A revision or working-group adoption before then would keep it alive.</p>
<p>Behind it sits the IETF's AI Preferences working group (AIPREF). That group has been working on a shared vocabulary for uses such as &ldquo;AI training,&rdquo; &ldquo;AI use&rdquo; and &ldquo;search,&rdquo; and on a new <code>Content-Usage</code> field to carry those preferences in robots.txt or HTTP headers. Its August 2026 target date has passed. The new draft takes the meaning of &ldquo;AI training&rdquo; from that vocabulary draft but borrows an ordinary robots.txt group instead of the new field, so it is usable today. It is closer to a detour. Whether four large companies honoring it first will make the detour the de facto standard is the thing to watch.</p>

<h3>This wiki's case: saying that training is welcome too</h3>
<p>This wiki was built from the start as a public, published wiki that AI can read and cite easily. Its <code>robots.txt</code> allows every crawler and also names 15 major AI crawlers to welcome them explicitly. The content license is CC BY 4.0, which permits free reuse with attribution.</p>
<p>Read under the new draft's rules, though, the file said <strong>nothing</strong> about training. <code>User-agent: *</code> does not match the token, and a group naming crawlers is not a token group. The intent was a welcome, but the machine-readable signal was &ldquo;no preference.&rdquo; So on November 2 this wiki added one more group.</p>
