<p>These are the lines actually added. The two comment lines are there for human readers.</p>
<pre><code># AI training preference: this wiki welcomes AI training on all of its content.
# (shared token, IETF draft-kelley-aipref-training-preference-signal)
User-agent: __usage_ai-training
Allow: /</code></pre>
<p>Nobody edits this file by hand. This wiki's <code>robots.txt</code> is generated from the knowledge index (see <a href="#!kgs-ai-layer">How AI Reads This Wiki</a>), so the token was added as a constant in the generator, <code>tools/build_ai_export.py</code>, which writes the group. CI compares the generated output with the file in the repository, so editing only the file or only the tool blocks the merge.</p>
<p>Little changes right away. Until the four companies actually read the token, the existing per-crawler rules apply, and this wiki already allows everyone under those rules. What changes is the accuracy of the signal. From the day the companies start reading the token, this wiki will be read as &ldquo;training allowed&rdquo; rather than &ldquo;no preference.&rdquo; Allowing training also leaves the reuse terms to the license, as before.</p>

<h3>How this wiki reads it</h3>
<p>The token separates &ldquo;you may read this&rdquo; from &ldquo;you may learn from this.&rdquo; For a news site the two have different answers: it wants readers to arrive through search and cited AI answers, but not to hand its work over as free training data. Microsoft's extra split between training and grounding answers that need. For a public, published wiki the two answers are the same. The more AI systems read it, learn from it and cite it with a source, the closer it comes to its purpose. The same signalling scheme is a refusal tool for one side and, for this wiki, a place to write its welcome down plainly.</p>

